    14	## 1. Vision, problems and what is done differently
    15	
    16	### 1.1 The vision: a market world model
    17	
    18	Alex set the purpose when this architecture started: "the purpose of this system is to build a hedge fund brain, it is building the
    19	decison process for hedgefund analyst to source data and extract information (thesis) and link evidences for keeping it's dossier of
    20	events that impact the assets and events in the world" (2026-11-06).
    21	
    22	He now calls it a market world model (2026-11-16). In this document the phrase means one concrete thing: **a standing, dated record of
    23	the open investment questions about each company and theme, which only new information moves and which closes each
    24	question on its due day.** For each question
    25	the record holds:
    26	
    27	- **The question, fixed when it opened**, with its possible answers and its due day written before any evidence is judged. Alex:
    28	  "一条观点一定是有一个明确的 ladder 和到期日，这样才构成一个观点呀" (D38).
    29	- **Every statement that bears on it**: who said it, when, where it was reported, and every later report or revision of it.
    30	- **The current answer and its history.** The answer moves only when new information arrives. Alex: "有相关的信息抽取出现，自然会影响整个系统抽取的thesis 向前" (D13).
    31	- **The final answer on the due day.** Alex: "最后到期日的状态就定格就好" (D54).
    32	
    33	The whole system builds, updates and retires that record. Alex: "其实整个system做的事情就是建构，更新，删除这个data structure" (D26).
    34	
    35	**One picture.** On NVIDIA, a report says it may back OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio. The record opens one question:
    36	"Will NVIDIA sign a guarantee or backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease by 2027-12-31?" Its answers are fixed at opening: it
    37	happened, it is ruled out, or still open; due 2027-12-31. The amount of the guarantee is listed as a part not judged. Every later
    38	report of the same story joins one group instead of counting again. A company denial is filed as a later version of the report. The
    39	answer moves only on a statement that changes it. On the due day the answer is closed and kept.
    40	
    41	**Why a reader uses it.** Alex: "it's like a dossier, right? Or it's like a reference where people can anchor their beliefs, or they
    42	can check whatever they're thinking, whether it's already priced in, or, you know, how single factors evolve. I don't think the value
    43	is in, like, having an idea and basically just constructing alpha" (2026-10-22). The gap it closes: "大部分的人, 每一天都会有很多信息,
    44	但这些信息怎么变成交易? … 你要拆解这个信息, 理解它背后的驱动力啊, 哪些因子会影响他 … 这个才是真正中间转移的痛点" (2026-10-22). On a
    45	page: "一个用户的thesis 可以直接被拆解成可以理解的证据，并且看得到证据过去变化的历史" (2026-10-24).
    46	
    47	**What it is not.**
    48	- Not a tracker for a view the user already holds. Alex rejected that framing: "我只是把这个东西当成一个跟踪工具, 我就是完全是不对的呀"
    49	  (2026-10-22). The system forms the questions itself, before any user asks.
    50	- Not a price forecast or an alpha engine (his words above).
    51	- Not yet a judge of what is priced in. The record keeps the expectations it reads (a consensus estimate, a company's guidance) as
    52	  evidence, and a figure's ranges may be set against a consensus (section 7). But no step compares a judged answer with what the
    53	  market already prices, so the record cannot by itself say whether an answer is in the price (section 10).
    54	
    55	### 1.2 The problems it answers
    56	
    57	Three things make an analyst's job hard, and the record is built against each:
    58	
    59	1. **Most of what arrives is noise; the increment is rare.** Alex: "不是什么事情都是惊弓之鸟需要抽取，杂音还是很大的，我们整体应该倾向谨慎…同时我们要敏感一些新的信息带来的增量不要错过" (D243).
    60	   The system keeps few statements and only big questions. Alex: "我觉得核心就是这个颗粒度的把控要符合一个高端的hedge fund analyst 的思维。不要搜集一堆鸡毛蒜皮的事情，要看重要的大事，合并整合关键的信息" (D248).
    61	2. **One statement arrives many times.** The same forecast comes through many outlets and reposts. Counted as many, it fakes
    62	   conviction. The record keeps it as one statement with all its sources (section 6).
    63	3. **An answer written from scratch has no memory and no score.** It cannot say what it concluded two weeks ago, what changed it, or
    64	   whether it was right. The record fixes the question and its answers first, dates every judgement with its evidence, and closes the
    65	   answer on the due day.
    66	
    67	**Why one model call over everything does not do this.** Alex, on asking a general model to research theses: "it's actually very, very
    68	hard because it hallucinates, it, you know, it doesn't really work" (2026-10-22). Two measurements point at what breaks:
    69	- **Too much material in one request fails.** In a three-month run the data-center revenue question held 1,768 linked pieces. Its
    70	  judging request was 292 KB with 698 evidence ids. The judge mislabelled ids, its answers were refused, and whole days halted (D358;
    71	  Alex: "bloating evidence 的问题 … 很严重，需要解决").
    72	- **The reasoning is not the bottleneck; delivering the right facts to the right question is.** The custom-chip question, sent as it
    73	  was, answered "below $40 billion" 9 times of 9. With the Broadcom and Trainium facts the ledger already held added, it left the
    74	  lowest range 9 times of 9 and reasoned as the expert readers did (`docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md`, Evidence).
    75	
    76	So the architecture puts its effort into reading, filing and grouping, and keeps the judge small and fixed. No test has compared the
    77	system with a single general-model call on the same questions; the case rests on the measurements above.
    78	
