    19	| Questions that are trivia or duplicates, both teams | not read | one draw each: new 1 of 7, old 6 of 15 | **none** |
    20	| Answers supported by what the judge was shown, both teams | trial, one draw each: new 7 of 9, old 10 of 12 | one draw each: new 6 of 7, old 10 of 15 | **none** |
    21	| Cited statements whose summary departs from the passage the judge was shown (neither reading checked them against the full articles) | trial: 4 of 201 flagged by both teams (the two systems together, unconfirmed) | one in each system | none |
    22	| Delivery (facts that reached the judge of their question) | trial, the gold's 62 facts, one draw each: delivered new 31, old 22; never delivered though judged later: new 8, old 19; no known "would change" control | the new system only: three confirmed pairs, 23 open (D508) | none |
    23	| Agreement with the frozen reference, seven graded questions | **three draws each**: new 2, 1, 0; old 1, 1, 1 | not applicable | the only reader-based line with repeats: the new system's own spread (0 to 2) is wider than the gap between systems |
    24	| By code: questions opened | new 9, 8, 12; old 12, 13, 14 | new 7; old 15 | three draws each in July |
    25	| By code: statements with no question (new) | 347, 286, 310 of about 550 | 382 of 567 | three draws in July |
    26	| By code: share of links on the largest question | new 0.20, 0.25, 0.20; old 0.53, 0.32, 0.39 | new 0.26; old 0.28 | July: the ranges do not overlap; the fresh week shows no gap |
    27	
    28	Sources: `TRIAL-RESULT.md`; `week/WEEK-RESULT.md`; `clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/CENSUS-FULL-RESULT.md`; `draw-counts.json`.
    29	
    30	What this table says before any calculation:
    31	- For the three reader-based lines a choice would rest on (matters covered, question quality, answers supported) there is **one draw
    32	  of each system on each of two windows, and no repeat**: the spread between draws of one system is unmeasured for them.
    33	- Where repeats exist (the frozen reference; counts by code), a system's own spread is as wide as the gap between systems, except
    34	  for the share of links piled on one question in July, and that gap is absent in the fresh week.
    35	- On answers supported, the sign of the difference flips between the two windows (6 points for the old system, then 19 for the new).
    36	
    37	## 2. How large a difference a comparison could detect
    38	
    39	Inputs (all from the fresh week, and all estimates): at the end of one week the new system holds 7 questions with a latest answer
    40	and the old system 15; a week costs about $2.52 a draw for the new system (charged) and about $0.50 for the old (its token count at
    41	listed rates, rounded up; not a separately charged amount). The article database holds articles for three later weeks (2026-10-06
    42	to 10-26); **no exposure census has been run on them and no article list priced**, so their being unread and their cost are
    43	assumptions here. The week of 09-29 has 40 articles already read once by the reading step.
    44	
    45	The smallest true difference detected four times in five (two-sided, 5%):
    46	
    47	| Shape | Cost | Answers supported | Trivia or duplicates |
    48	|---|---|---|---|
    49	| 1 week, 3 draws each | about $9 | 31 points if repeated draws were as good as new weeks; 54 if they add nothing | 34; 60 |
    50	| 2 weeks, 3 draws each | about $18 | 22; 38 | 24; 42 |
    51	| 3 weeks, 1 draw each | about $9 | 31 | 34 |
    52	| 3 weeks, 2 draws each | about $18 | 22; 31 | 24; 34 |
    53	| 4 weeks, 3 draws each (more unread news and money than there is) | about $36 | 16; 27 | 17; 30 |
    54	
    55	- The calculation assumes independent answers, fixed weekly question counts and particular starting shares. Questions within a
    56	  week, and repeated draws of the same week, are correlated to a degree the stored runs do not measure for these readings. "As good
    57	  as new weeks" is an upper bound on what repeated draws can give.
    58	- The simulation checks the formula under the same assumptions; it does not validate the assumptions. For 3 weeks and 1 draw (21
    59	  and 45 answers): a 31-point difference is detected in 82% of 20,000 simulated comparisons, a 10-point difference in 12%, and a
    60	  20-point difference in roughly 40 to 60% depending on the starting shares (about 37% at 83 against 63, about 57% at 97 against
    61	  77, the council's own runs).
    62	- Big matters covered cannot be sized this way at all: a week gives two to seven such matters.
    63	
    64	## 3. The stop condition of D516, applied
    65	
    66	- **A stated difference the comparison is meant to detect:** none can be defended. Under the simple assumptions the shapes the
    67	  money allows detect differences of roughly 22 to 38 points or more; one draw of each system does not tell us whether the true
    68	  differences are that large (the largest seen is 26 points, on one week), and on the one line with two windows the sign flips.
    69	- **A defensible size and cost for it:** the reliability of a $9 to $18 comparison cannot be calculated from the stored readings
    70	  (no repeated draws of the reader-based lines; correlation unmeasured). Spending the remaining money on a test whose power is
    71	  unknown and plausibly low is not defensible.
    72	- **The readers' controls:** the delivery reading still has no known single-fact "would change" case (the six calls of D511 did not
    73	  give one), so that line could not be exercised either.
    74	- **So this design is not sent.** What is unmeasured and would have to change for a comparison to decide: many more independent
    75	  weeks of unread news than the database holds (seven to nine weeks for a 20-point difference at one draw each), or a line that does
    76	  not depend on the handful of questions a week produces.
    77	
    78	## 4. What this means for the decision page
    79	
    80	The page's section 5 offered Alex "one comparison written down before it runs" as the way to choose by measurement. The page now
    81	says, in plain words: at the proposed size the comparison may miss differences that matter; we cannot yet calculate its
    82	reliability from our stored readings; we did not run it.
    83	
    84	## 5. Council (reflection thread "my", round 7): consensus, with corrections
    85	
    86	The council ran the script and reproduced its figures, and corrected this page's first wording: "only about 30 points or more" (the
    87	table gives 22 to 38), "nothing seen suggests a 30-point difference" (one draw does not establish it), the single 40% figure for a
    88	20-point gap (it depends on the starting shares), the row that claimed a check of cited statements against their articles (both
    89	readings used the passages shown to the judge), and the unread weeks and costs stated as facts (they are estimates until an
    90	exposure census and an article list exist). **Decided under D439 (D517): this comparison is not sent.**
     1	# Can a same-news product comparison from established libraries decide anything? (D610's review), 2026-11-16, $0
     2	
     3	**Answer: not within what is left.** It closes by D610's own condition.
     4	
     5	1. **Power.** D517's sizing (`comparison/COMPARISON-SIZING.md`) stands. A week ends with about 7 questions on the new system and 15 on
     6	   the old.
     7	   - The shapes about $9 buys detect a difference of about 31 points or more in answers supported, under assumptions the stored
     8	     readings cannot check.
     9	   - Big matters covered cannot be sized this way: a week gives two to seven.
    10	   - D439 has about $10 left.
    11	   - A starting library changes where questions come from, not how many a week produces, so it does not change this arithmetic.
    12	2. **Comparable libraries.** The old system's library ends at 2026-08-28 (the sealed three-month run, D206). The new system has
    13	   carried libraries to 10-05, 10-12 and 10-19. A same-day start needs the old system replayed from 08-29 for about seven weeks.
    14	   - That is about $3.50 at D517's estimate of $0.50 a week, unpriced by token count here.
    15	   - It would leave about $6 for the comparison itself.
    16	3. **Unread news.** By the stored manifests, every week from 2026-09-21 to 10-26 has been read by at least one run of the new system
    17	   or by Step 1's development runs. The week of 10-19 appears in the takeover, unfiled, capture-control and full-page diagnostic
    18	   runs. So no exposure-free week remains for a fresh comparison, and the earlier assumption that three later weeks were unread
    19	   (D517) does not hold.
    20	
    21	**So:** a comparison now would be underpowered, and partly built on news the new system's development has seen. Nothing is run. This
    22	does not show the systems equal. What would change it:
    23	- more unread news than the database holds;
