    44	     fresh material and his reading time to resolve a meaningful difference, and a written rule for what favours either system or leaves it
    45	     unclear. It is reviewed by the council and run only on his word.
    46	   - If no such design clears the earlier feasibility objections (`comparison-feasibility/RESULT.md`, D612), that finding goes to him.
    47	   - The stop hook must accept the hold; that needs his word on the standing goal.
    48	
    49	Answer CONSENSUS or DISAGREE, with the smallest change.
     1	**CONSENSUS. Put Deck 1 to Alex today, unchanged.** The result decides whether to design a fair comparison. For example, if Alex catches both planted wrong answers and marks the repeats consistently, that supports designing the comparison. It leaves the choice of system open. [Deck design](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/pilot-deck-1/DESIGN.md:64)
     2	
     3	The only wording change I’d make is in the handoff: **“While Alex’s marks are pending, hold the work listed in Stop. Ask Alex separately whether to defer D658.”** That keeps the general hold from silently overriding his approval of the reference work. Ask him to make that hold explicit in the standing goal so the stop hook can honor it. [D658](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:9208) · [Stop-hook record](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:8803)
