docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-8/narrow-d436/audit-v51-codex.md:14:   - “**not several things with none the main one: ask the figure, and list that second side**” is **consistent** with D654’s explicit exception. It qualifies the no-main-part decline in [system line 6](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-8/narrow-d436/edited-system.txt:6) and the [no_ladder description](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-schema.json:19); it does not remove the other decline reasons. The [closing check](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-closing.txt:1) still asks for one outcome-named thing and every omitted part, so it is compatible.
docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-8/narrow-d436/audit-v55-codex.md:9:| “When the outcome tracks a figure and also names one of these second sides of the same bet … ask the figure, and list that second side” | **Consistent** | This states [D654](</Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:9093>) and carries [D436](</Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:4847>) into the display required by [D397](</Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:4326>). It specifically qualifies line 6’s decline for several things with none main. |
docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-8/narrow-d436/audit-v55-codex.md:18:The existing sentence “Never fold two things into one ladder” is compatible: the request still judges one figure and lists the other side. The unmodified line 6 says to decline several things with none main; the new sentence explicitly says the three specified shapes are not that case. The remaining date, period and subject restrictions still apply. The [closing text](</Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-closing.txt>) matches the supplied frozen closing apart from its final newline; the supplied schema pointer changes the stated description.
docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-8/narrow-d436/audit-v52-codex.md:12:| “the two are one bet … not several things with none the main one” | **Consistent.** | D654 explicitly says these three shapes are not the no-main-part decline case. This qualifies system line 6 and the matching `no_ladder` description; it does not remove the other decline reasons. |
docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-8/narrow-d436/audit-v52-codex.md:20:The main sentence does not collide with the closing check or `no_ladder` when the request is read as a whole: D654 identifies the figure as the question for the specified shapes, while line 6 still declines other no-main topics. The due-date and ownership rules remain applicable.
docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-8/narrow-d436/audit-v53-codex.md:11:| “When the outcome tracks a figure and also names one of these second sides of the same bet … ask the figure, and list that second side in not_judged.” | **Consistent with D654.** It also implements D397’s requirement to list the parts left out. The phrase “not several things with none the main one” is an express exception to the system’s decline sentence on line 6. But the schema’s `no_ladder` description still independently says to decline “several with none the main one,” without that exception. The closing check likewise asks for “the one the topic’s outcome names” without explaining D654’s choice when the outcome names both sides without priority. These are request-level collisions. |
docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-8/narrow-d436/audit-v53-codex.md:30:For each, the schema and line 6 leave a competing literal instruction to decline when the outcome names both without saying which is main. Thus the table states the result **under the inserted D654 instruction**, not a guarantee that the complete request produces it.
